HolRefute provides counterexample generation for HOL4, with theorem
certification enabled by default.  It has a Quickcheck (QC) backend for
executable goals, native narrowing, and a Kodkod-based model-finder (MF)
backend.  Exhaustive and random QC testing run over three interchangeable
execution substrates:

* Compute interprets test plans with computeLib.  It is the compatibility
  substrate and supports registered custom and abstract generators.
* Cv compiles a fuel-bounded HOL test loop and executes it with
  Thm.compute.  Goals outside its first-order fragment are inapplicable.
* NativeSML extracts the executable fragment to SML and compiles the test
  loop in process.  It is the preferred fast substrate on Poly/ML.

Set a substrate with `Refute.upd_substrate`.  The default, `Auto`, walks all
registered substrates in priority order; the built-in order is NativeSML,
Cv, then Compute.  Auto falls through when a substrate is inapplicable.  An
explicitly selected inapplicable substrate reports `Unknown`; it never
silently changes substrate.  Selection applies to both exhaustive and random
QC backends.  A custom or replacement substrate can open a smart-generator
gate only by compiling the smart plan itself, and Auto executes that exact
selected compilation.  At Refute trace level 2 or higher, Auto reports
selection and fallthrough reasons.  Every counterexample also records the
selected substrate in its `substrate` field.

Random testing uses one specified 64-bit generator and consumption order on
all substrates.  `Refute.upd_seed (SOME n)` therefore reproduces the same
candidate stream independently of substrate.  Runs without an explicit
seed use a deterministic session stream which advances between runs.

Smart generators are enabled by default.  A `Hol_reln` premise, or a
Boolean function with exhaustive Horn-shaped equations, is mode-compiled to
a positive CPS enumerator for exhaustive testing on all three substrates.
`upd_depth` sets its fixed per-plan fuel (default 10); it does not add a
schedule loop.  Compute and cv define the same structurally recursive HOL
list enumerators (cv then translates those definitions); native SML preserves
the same clause and premise order.  Because fuel bounds every smart
enumeration, Enum- or SmartGuard-bearing terminal exhaustion is always
reported as incomplete.  Ignored counterexamples are matched by structural
environment equality, not by stream position.  Setting
`upd_smart_generators false` restores generate-and-guard planning, and
`upd_reorder_premises` controls mode-analysis reordering.

Native narrowing is active by default as the `narrowing` backend.  It uses
`size` as its refinement-depth bound and supports prenex existential goals.
`upd_finite_functions true` (the default) represents function inputs by
finite `ffun` update chains, with `cfun` as the constant-only fallback for
higher-order domains.  Counterexamples reconstruct `ffun` values as HOL
`UPDATE` chains.  Setting the option to false restores the direct function-
type inapplicability diagnostic.

Execution substrates are untrusted accelerators.  When `certify=true` (the
default), an exhaustive or random QC candidate becomes `Genuine` only after
Refute_Cert re-evaluates its instantiated proposition with computeLib and
constructs a theorem certifying its negation.  Native narrowing uses
Refute_Cert_Narrow to replay its recorded case tree; ground and partial
candidates fall back to Refute_Cert evaluation.  With `certify=false`,
Refute skips that QC
certification: a QC hit otherwise eligible for `Genuine` remains `Genuine`,
but has `cert = NONE` and is reported as uncertified.  Disabling certification
does not promote candidates already known to be stuck or incomplete.
`certainty` records the semantic strength of the result, while `cert` records
whether Refute constructed a HOL theorem.  The two are independent axes: a
result may be `Genuine` with `cert = NONE`.

SETTLED DECISION — do not revisit without the owner.  Refute trusts Kodkodi
exactly as Isabelle/HOL's Nitpick trusts Kodkod, and must never be stricter.
A model-finder verdict is decided by the *encoding*, not by whether a HOL
theorem was built: `Refute_ModelFinder_Model.fallback_certainty` maps the
translation's soundness and exactness flags onto `Genuine`, `QuasiGenuine`
and `Potential`, mirroring Nitpick's genuine / quasi-genuine / potentially
spurious.  A reconstructed model may therefore be `Genuine` with
`cert = NONE`, exactly as a QC hit is under `certify=false`.  Requiring a
certificate for a genuine model-finder result collapses the two axes, makes
the model finder inconsistent with the QC path, and starves the
`max_potential` budget so that found models are discarded and reported as
`Unknown`.  That rule was introduced on 2026-07-29 (3948a7bed), its
consequences re-baselined into the tests (2db19131b), and both were reverted;
tests expecting `Genuine` with `cert = NONE` from kodkod are correct as
written.  The same rule reappeared as a budget (92c24de7e) and was removed
too: `max_potential` bounds models that may be spurious because their
*encoding* is unsound, so only the liberal problems spend it.  Every model
of a sound problem — quasi-genuine because the encoding is inexact, or
merely `Potential` because kernel certification got stuck — is a real
result and is charged to `max_genuine`.
Refute uses no oracle tags.  Cv performs all per-call definitions and
translations inside a full theory snapshot/revert bracket.  Success,
failure, timeout, and interruption paths leave no Refute-created type,
constant, theorem, or binding in the user's theory; if safe cleanup is
unavailable, Cv reports that the goal is inapplicable instead.

Backend races stop only when a result reaches the highest certainty that
any selected backend declares reachable for the current configuration and
instances.  `Refute.register_backend` gives existing backends the safe,
conservative ceiling `Genuine`.  A backend with a tighter bound can use
`Refute.register_backend_with_ceiling`.  Its callback must return an upper
bound: overestimating only forgoes an early stop, whereas underestimating can
hide a stronger result and violates the backend contract.

Racing also needs a session thread count above one.  It is set by the
Holmake and build `--mt` flag, or directly by
`Multithreading.max_threads_update`, and defaults to one; at one the
parallel walk over the selected backends degrades to a sequential one.
The default `upd_sequential false` therefore runs the backends one at a
time unless threads are enabled.  Only the schedule differs, never the
result; Refute reports the degradation at trace level 2 or higher.

The unused-assumption facade consists of `check_unused_assms`,
`find_unused_assms`, and `print_unused_assms`.  Each takes a `config option`
first.  `NONE` derives a QC-only configuration from the current configuration;
`SOME cfg` supplies the base configuration and preserves its backend selection.
Every individual probe forces a sequential, quiet, abort-potential,
expectation-free profile.

User interface
--------------

`REFUTE_TAC` is the normal proof-development interface.  It runs every
selected backend, prints the result, and returns the original goal unchanged:

    Proof
      REFUTE_TAC >>
      (* prove the unchanged goal *)
    QED

Three specialized tactics restrict only the search selection inherited from
`the_config`:

* `QUICKCHECK_TAC` selects exhaustive, random, and narrowing search;
* `NARROWING_TAC` selects only native symbolic narrowing; and
* `MODEL_REFUTE_TAC` selects only the relational model finder.

Most proof scripts should use `REFUTE_TAC`.  The specialized names are useful
when a particular counterexample shape or backend capability is wanted.

Advanced callers can change options for one tactic application without
mutating `the_config`:

    REFUTE_TAC_WITH
      [upd_search (Only [Narrowing]),
       upd_size 7,
       upd_timeout 5.0]

The elements are ordinary `config -> config` updaters and are applied from
left to right, so the last update of a field wins.  `REFUTE_TAC_WITH` reads
`the_config` when the tactic is applied.  `REFUTE_CONFIG_TAC cfg` instead
uses exactly `cfg`, which is preferable for reproducible tools and tests.
Neither form silently changes `quiet` or `expect`.

Backend selection is typed for the four built-ins:

    datatype backend_choice =
        Exhaustive
      | Random
      | Narrowing
      | ModelFinder
      | RegisteredBackend of string

    datatype search =
        AllBackends
      | QuickcheckBackends
      | Only of backend_choice list

`RegisteredBackend` is the escape hatch for an extension registered under a
string name.  `upd_backends` remains available for source compatibility and
low-level registry code.

The same per-call update list works outside the tactic layer through
`refute_with` and `refute_goal_with`.  `apply_updates updates cfg` constructs
an exact configuration, while `current_config updates` applies them to the
current interactive configuration.

Module layout:

* Refute_Core: shared types, configuration, registry, and orchestration.
* Refute_Gen: generator derivation and enumeration support.
* Refute_Eval: the substrate seam, registry, and common random generator.
* Refute_EvalCompute, Refute_EvalCv, and Refute_EvalSML: substrates.
* Refute_Extract: in-memory HOL-to-SML extraction for NativeSML.
* Refute_QC: QC test planning and random/exhaustive execution.
* Refute_QC_Narrow: native narrowing compilation and scheduled execution.
* Refute_Cert: EVAL-based counterexample certification.
* Refute_Cert_Narrow: narrowing case-tree validation and proof replay.
* Refute_Forl and Refute_ForlSat: the FORL/Kodkodi process bridge and SAT
  solver configuration.
* Refute_ModelFinder and Refute_ModelFinder_*: preprocessing, scope and
  representation selection, Kodkod translation, and model reconstruction.
* Refute_Unused: unused-assumption probes and theory sweeps.
* Refute.sig and Refute.sml: the public API facade.

The `examples/` directory holds executable theory scripts covering the whole
user-facing surface, from first steps to the model finder, the extension
registries, and which library types each half of Refute can serve;
`examples/README` indexes them.  Run `Holmake examples` here to build them
with the standard HOL heap and their declared `Ancestors` and `Libs`.

Model-finder surface
--------------------

The model finder supports native polymorphic scopes, monotonicity and smart
finitization, specialization and boxing, binary integers, (co)inductive
predicates, codatatypes, quotients, typedefs, value pinning, model formatting,
and incremental multi-model search.  The principal options and their defaults
are:

| updater | default | meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `upd_max` | `[(NONE, [~1])]` | constructor carrier bound; `~1` unbounded |
| `upd_specialize` | `true` | specialize static higher-order arguments |
| `upd_box` | `[(NONE, NONE)]` | box functions and pairs when useful |
| `upd_binary_ints` | `NONE` | select binary integers when useful |
| `upd_bits` | `[1, ..., 10]` | candidate binary word widths |
| `upd_star_linear_preds` | `true` | use exact closure for linear predicates |
| `upd_iter` | default depth row (below) | fixpoint unrolling depths |
| `upd_bisim_depth` | `[9]` | codatatype bisimulation depth; `~1` disables it |
| `upd_finitize` | `[(NONE, NONE)]` | finitization selected by monotonicity |
| `upd_whack` | `[]` | constants whose definitions are ignored |
| `upd_need` | `NONE` | values that must occur in the model |
| `upd_tac_timeout` | `0.5` seconds | inner tactic budget; `0` skips |
| `upd_format` | fallback `[1]` | group curried function arguments |
| `upd_max_potential` | `1` | maximum potential models returned |
| `upd_max_genuine` | `1` | maximum genuine models returned |
| `upd_merge_type_vars` | `false` | fuse all MF type-variable scopes |

The default `iter` row is `[0,1,2,4,8,12,16,20,24,28]`.  A `NONE` key in a
row is its fallback.  For smart Boolean rows, a `NONE` value asks Refute to
choose; `SOME true` and `SOME false` force the choice.  A `max` row is keyed
by constructor rather than by type: it bounds how many values that
constructor may contribute to its type's carrier.
`tac_timeout` bounds the two inner proof attempts the model finder makes
per problem: the monotonicity calculus, and the well-foundedness proof for
an inductive predicate.  Either one exceeding it is not an error; the
answer degrades to "not monotonic" or "not well-founded", which costs
scopes or precision but never soundness.  A budget of `0` is decided
rather than timed, so it skips both attempts outright instead of racing
them against a deadline that has already passed.
The `max_potential` and `max_genuine` limits are global budgets.  Requesting
more than one model forces an incremental SAT solver when necessary, and a
genuine result ends the potential-model search.  With
`upd_merge_type_vars true`, the model finder type-instantiates every goal,
evaluated-term, and needed-term type variable to the alphabetically first one
before building its context.  This reduces independent scope blocks, at the
cost of potentially losing models that require the variables to have
different cardinalities.

Registrations are session-local ML state and create no theory content:

* `Refute.register_codatatype {tyop, case_const, constructors}` validates or
  refreshes an audited built-in codatatype registration.  User-defined
  codatatypes cannot yet be registered, because their coinduction witness is
  not part of this API.  Lazy built-ins cover `llist`, `ltree`, `itree`,
  `itreeTau`, `lbtree`, and `path` when their theories are present.
* `Refute.register_quotient {qty, rty, abs, rep, equiv_thm, partial}`
  registers the abstract and representation types and constants.  `equiv_thm`
  must be hypothesis-free and either a `QUOTIENT equiv abs rep` theorem
  mentioning the registered `abs` and `rep`, or a total equivalence
  `!x y. R x y <=> (R x = R y)`.  Its shape, not the legacy `partial` field,
  selects the encoding: a `QUOTIENT` theorem gets the sound partial
  encoding, whereas a total equivalence drops its then redundant domain
  axiom.  The `partial` field is ignored and retained only for source
  compatibility.
* `Refute.register_typedef {ty, abs, rep, absrep_thm}` registers an existing
  HOL typedef.  `absrep_thm` must be its exported conjunction of the two
  bijection laws and must agree with the type's `<tyop>_TY_DEF` theorem.
* `Refute.register_term_postprocessor pattern f` installs a bottom-up,
  display-only model-value transformation for every type matched by
  `pattern`; `f` must preserve the term's type.  Re-registering the same
  pattern replaces rather than duplicates it.  All matching transformations
  compose: general patterns run before strictly more specific patterns, and
  otherwise older registrations run before newer ones.  Thus a specific or
  later incomparable transformation has final precedence.  One registry
  snapshot is used for an entire model, callback failures or type changes
  leave that display term unchanged, and certification always uses untouched
  raw terms.  `Refute.lookup_term_postprocessor ty` reports this composed
  transformation.  Rational Frac encoding is registered by default after its
  normalization-faithfulness corpus passed.  The idempotent
  `Refute.register_frac_type_rat ()` restores or refreshes both the encoding
  and the built-in transformation, which displays reconstructed Frac atoms
  with `rat_cons`'s `//` syntax.  Example 12 works the
  encoding through: what the shorthand registers, what an incomplete ersatz
  table costs, and what restoring it looks like.  Support for `real` via
  Frac is deferred; certification of real literals is a real blocker.

Structural registration types use distinct type variables as parameters.
Abs, rep, case, and constructor terms must be existing constants.  A later
compatible registration for the same type operator replaces the session
entry.

Kodkodi support is Unix-only and requires a Java runtime plus the standard
Unix `setsid` utility (used to isolate and clean up its process group).  Obtain
`kodkodi-1.5.7.tar.gz` from the Isabelle components mirror at
<https://isabelle.in.tum.de/components/> and unpack it locally.  Set
`HOL4_KODKODI` to the resulting `kodkodi-1.5.7` component directory.  Its
`jar/` directory must contain `antlr-runtime-3.1.1.jar`, `kodkod-1.5.jar`,
`kodkodi-1.5.7.jar`, and `sat4j-2.3.jar`.

HolRefute constructs the Kodkodi JVM command itself.  Java is selected from
`HOL4_JAVA_EXECUTABLE`, then `JAVA_HOME/bin/java`, then `java` on `PATH`.
As an escape hatch, `HOL4_KODKODI_EXEC` can instead name a complete
standalone launcher command.  It takes precedence and is passed to the
shell verbatim.  Configuration checks are lazy and never start a JVM while
modules are loaded or built.  The launcher shipped in the component's
`bin/` directory depends on Isabelle and is not the standalone override.

`SAT4J` is the portable default.  JNI solvers are advertised only when their
individual shared libraries are present under `jni/<platform>`: MiniSat,
CryptoMiniSat, and Lingeling require `libminisat`, `libcryptominisat`, and
`liblingeling`, respectively.  External solver settings retain Isabelle's
environment variables, including `MINISAT_HOME`, `CRYPTOMINISAT_HOME`,
`ZCHAFF_HOME`, `RSAT_HOME`, `RISS3G_HOME`, `BERKMIN_HOME`, and
`BERKMINALLOY_HOME`; `BERKMIN_EXE` selects a nondefault BerkMin executable.

The prepared development host uses
`$HOME/.local/opt/Isabelle2025-2/contrib/kodkodi-1.5.7` and
`$HOME/.local/bin/java`; other machines need a local Kodkodi 1.5.7 component
and a Java runtime.  No network access is needed at run time.

HolRefute is included in the normal tree build only on Poly/ML; non-Poly
builds skip the directory.  Its level-2 selftests include the MF acceptance,
differential, soundness, and theory-hygiene suites, plus cross-substrate QC
outcome and candidate-stream conformance and Cv export-cleanliness checks.

The refute_simp, refute_psimp, and refute_unfold theorem sets are defined by
the support theory and used by model-finder preprocessing.

`Refute.upd_iter` selects model-finder unrolling depths.  A mutual fixpoint
group shares one iterator row: members are checked in `_cases` theorem order,
and the first member with an explicit row wins before the default row is used.
Predicates introduced by `Hol_reln` and `Hol_coreln` are recognized through
the IndDef registries and support direct well-founded equations or joint
least/greatest-fixpoint unrolling.  Hand-rolled greatest fixpoints are not
recognized; define coinductive predicates with `Hol_coreln`.

Known limitations and follow-ups
--------------------------------

The model finder lazily harvests typedef and quotient metadata when an
unregistered Abs/Rep occurrence is encountered.  Loading or exporting a theory
only records its invalidated harvest entry; pending theories are indexed on
first demand.  If validated harvesting fails, it returns `Unknown` rather than
risk a spurious genuine result; an explicit registration can then supply the
metadata.  Harvesting is deliberately demand-driven rather than an eager scan
on every theory load.

The normalized Frac/ersatz encoding for `rat` is registered by default.
Support for `real` via Frac remains deferred because certification of real
literals is unresolved.  Relation inverse and composition (`inv` and `O`)
already have native monotonicity rules, with dedicated verdict, scope,
soundness, and certified argument-order coverage.  Relation identity (`Id`)
remains unfolded because there is no corresponding nut builtin.

Recorded follow-ups are compute-hosted residual-inspection narrowing; full
function flattening; higher-order and negative smart-generator modes; `real`
via Frac; offset interval rewrites such as `lo <= n /\\ n < hi`; and eager
whole-ancestry typedef/quotient harvesting.  These are not part of the current
implementation.
